From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stephen Bates <sbates@raithlin.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 21:33:01 -0500
Subject: [RFC PATCH 3/8] ionic: trust the device's L4 checksum on header split queues

ionic reports CHECKSUM_COMPLETE, from the sum the device puts in the
completion, and uses the OK and BAD flags only for a counter. That is
the better choice as long as the stack can read the packet: it works for
any protocol and any encapsulation.

It stops working when the payload is in memory the kernel can't read,
which is what header split is for. Whenever the stack has to adjust the
sum, it reads the bytes it removes (pskb_trim_rcsum() for a frame with
padding, for example), and skb_checksum() on an unreadable skb warns
and returns nothing useful. Of the drivers that can receive into
unreadable memory, bnxt and gve report CHECKSUM_UNNECESSARY, and fbnic
does when the device vouches for the L4 checksum.

On a queue with IONIC_RXQ_F_HDS, report CHECKSUM_UNNECESSARY when the
device says that it verified the TCP or UDP checksum, and keep
CHECKSUM_COMPLETE for everything else. Queues without header split,
which is all of them until a later patch, behave as before.

Move the checksum code to a helper while at it, and count the new case.
